# 16. Common Architecture Mistakes

## Putting everything into one script

A monolithic automation script may be easy to start with but becomes difficult to maintain as requirements grow.

## Mixing content and execution

Content generation and browser execution have different failure modes and should not depend unnecessarily on each other.

## No persistent task state

Without state, recovering from crashes can create duplicate work.

## No centralized monitoring

When every worker logs differently, identifying systemic problems becomes difficult.

## Treating every account identically

Accounts may have different configurations, workloads, histories, and operational requirements.

## Scaling before measuring

Adding more workers does not solve a problem that originates in scheduling, content, network connectivity, or account configuration.

